<GuideLayout
  title="URL Encoding Explained: How Percent-Encoding Actually Works"
  description="A plain-language guide to URL encoding (percent-encoding): why it exists, how bytes become %XX, how spaces turn into %20 or +, and how UTF-8 handles non-ASCII text."
  relatedTools={relatedTools}
  datePublished="2026-09-06"
  dateModified="2026-09-06"
>
  <p>
    When you paste a search query into a browser, the address bar often fills with things like
    <code>%20</code>, <code>%C3%A9</code>, or <code>q%3Dhello</code>. That is URL encoding, also called
    percent-encoding. This guide explains what those codes mean, why they appear, and how the encoding is
    computed step by step. The diagrams below run the real algorithm in your browser, so you can type your
    own text and watch it get encoded.
  </p>

  <h2>What URL encoding actually is</h2>
  <p>
    URL encoding is a way to put characters into a URL that the URL syntax does not allow as-is. A URL is
    defined over a limited set of bytes. Anything outside that set, and a handful of characters that have
    special meaning inside a URL, must be rewritten so the URL stays valid and unambiguous.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rewrite is simple to describe: take the byte, convert it to two uppercase hexadecimal digits, and put
    a percent sign in front. The space byte <code>0x20</code> becomes <code>%20</code>. The letter
    <code>A</code> (byte <code>0x41</code>) would become <code>%41</code>, but because <code>A</code> is
    already allowed, it stays <code>A</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    One detail trips people up: URL encoding is <strong>not</strong> encryption and <strong>not</strong>
    compression. The original text is fully recoverable, and the result is usually longer than the input. It
    is a transport format, not a way to hide data.
  </p>

  <h2>Why URLs need encoding</h2>
  <p>
    A URL has structure. Characters such as <code>?</code>, <code>&amp;</code>, <code>=</code>,
    <code>/</code>, and <code>#</code> separate the path, query, and fragment. If the data you want to send
    contains one of those characters, the parser cannot tell whether it is part of your data or part of the
    URL structure. Encoding removes that ambiguity by turning the structural characters into inert
    <code>%XX</code> sequences.
  </p>
  <p>
    Encoding is also needed for characters that are not even legal in a URL at all, such as spaces, control
    characters, and most non-ASCII text. A raw space breaks the URL at the space, and a raw accented letter
    is not a valid byte in the ASCII URL grammar.
  </p>

  <h2>The core rule: encode the bytes, not the characters</h2>
  <p>
    The single most useful thing to understand is that URL encoding operates on <strong>bytes</strong>. The
    text is first turned into a sequence of bytes using a character encoding (almost always UTF-8), and then
    each byte that is not allowed becomes <code>%XX</code>. This matters most for non-English text, as the
    next sections show. For plain ASCII, the byte and the character are the same thing, so the difference is
    invisible.
  </p>

  <h2>Reserved versus unreserved characters</h2>
  <p>
    The set of characters that stay untouched is small and fixed by RFC 3986. It is called the unreserved
    set: the uppercase and lowercase letters <code>A</code>–<code>Z</code>, the digits
    <code>0</code>–<code>9</code>, and four symbols: hyphen <code>-</code>, period <code>.</code>,
    underscore <code>_</code>, and tilde <code>~</code>. Everything else printable, plus every control
    character, gets percent-encoded.
  </p>
  <p>
    Click around the grid to see which characters survive and which turn into codes:</p>
  <UrlReservedDemo />

  <h2>Walk through a full encoding</h2>
  <p>
    Type a string below and press play. The diagram encodes one character at a time, shows the UTF-8 byte or
    bytes for that character, and builds up the final percent-encoded string. Try <code>hello world</code>,
    then try something with punctuation.
  </p>
  <UrlEncoderVisualizer />

  <h2>Spaces: %20 in a URL, + in form data</h2>
  <p>
    Spaces are the most common source of confusion because there are <strong>two</strong> conventions. Inside
    a URL path or query string, a space is encoded as <code>%20</code>. But in
    <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> data, which is what a browser sends when a form is
    submitted with the default encoding, a space becomes a literal plus sign <code>+</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both forms are valid in their own context. The bug appears when they are mixed: a server that decodes
    form data expects <code>+</code> to mean space, while a server decoding a query string expects
    <code>%20</code>. If you send <code>+</code> into a query-string decoder, it may stay as a plus, and if
    you send <code>%20</code> into a form decoder, it works but looks unusual.
  </p>
  <p>Step through the same text encoded both ways to see exactly where they diverge:</p>
  <UrlFormVsQueryDemo />

  <h2>Non-ASCII text and UTF-8</h2>
  <p>
    URL encoding knows nothing about characters such as <code>é</code>, <code>中文</code>, or emoji. It only
    knows bytes. So before encoding, the text is converted to UTF-8. An ASCII character like
    <code>c</code> is one byte (<code>0x63</code>) and becomes one code (<code>%63</code>). The letter
    <code>é</code> is two UTF-8 bytes (<code>0xC3 0xA9</code>) and becomes two codes
    (<code>%C3%A9</code>). A Chinese character is three bytes, so it becomes three codes.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why a short non-English word can produce a long encoded string, and why the encoding is
    reproducible only if both sides agree on UTF-8. If the sender used a different legacy encoding, the bytes
    would differ and the receiver would decode garbage.
  </p>
  <UrlUtf8Demo />

  <h2>URL encoding versus HTML entities and Base64</h2>
  <p>
    Three encodings get mixed up because they all turn "weird" text into "safe" text.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>URL encoding</strong> targets URL syntax. It produces <code>%XX</code> and is meant for URLs
      and form fields.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>HTML entities</strong> target HTML markup. They produce <code>&amp;amp;</code> or
      <code>&amp;#233;</code> so a character shows up correctly inside an HTML document. The same character
      can look very different in the two systems. Our <a href="/guides/html-entities-guide/">HTML entities
      guide</a> explains the <code>&amp;lt;</code> and <code>&amp;amp;</code> rules in depth.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Base64</strong> targets binary data. It produces a blob of letters, digits, <code>+</code>,
      <code>/</code>, and <code>=</code> that is safe to drop into text, including a URL, but it is meant for
      arbitrary bytes, not for fixing URL syntax. Our <a href="/guides/base64-guide/">Base64 guide</a>
      covers it in depth.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A common pattern is to Base64-encode binary data and then URL-encode the Base64 result so it can sit in a
    query parameter. The two encodings stack; they are not alternatives to each other.
  </p>

  <h2>When you actually need it</h2>
  <p>
    You rarely call a URL encoder by hand. The platform does it for you:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      In JavaScript, <code>encodeURIComponent()</code> encodes a query value, and <code>encodeURI()</code>
      encodes a whole URL while leaving the structural characters alone. They are not interchangeable.
    </li>
    <li>
      In a browser, setting <code>form.enctype</code> to the default
      <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> makes the browser encode fields for you.
    </li>
    <li>
      On the server, every framework exposes a function to read query strings and form bodies back into
      normal strings.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You still need to understand it when the automatic encoding is missing, when you build URLs by string
    concatenation, or when data round-trips through more than one system. The usual failure is a value that
    contained <code>&amp;</code> or <code>=</code> and was never encoded, so the receiver split it into extra
    parameters.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Encoding the whole URL with <code>encodeURIComponent</code></strong> destroys the
      <code>?</code>, <code>&amp;</code>, and <code>=</code> that structure it. Encode the values, then
      assemble the URL.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Doubling the encoding</strong>. Encoding an already-encoded string turns <code>%20</code> into
      <code>%2520</code>, which decodes to a literal <code>%20</code> instead of a space.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mixing space conventions</strong>. Sending <code>+</code> where <code>%20</code> is expected
      leaves a plus in the data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Forgetting the character encoding</strong>. The same text encodes differently under UTF-8
      versus a legacy encoding, so agree on UTF-8 on both ends.
    </li>
  </ul>

